      "when The Water Knife first came out, its picture of a fractured, drought-stricken America was met with skepticism. critics called it overdrawn, too apocalyptic, and implausible for a nation used to abundance. but as the years have gone by, reality has taken on the very colours Bacigalupi imagined. resource scarcity, technological imbalance, and ecological unraveling, once thought speculative, now shape daily life. the novel's imagined dystopia has become a reflection of material fact, the product of choices both deliberate and deferred.",
