      "the novel's water, traded as both weapon and currency, now mirrors present policy. agricultural communities are left bereft not by accident but by hierarchy, where industrial need and urban privilege come first. because efficiency has become the ruling creed, farmers give up irrigation to keep cities running, and survival itself gets auctioned off under bureaucratic cover. here is the echo of Bacigalupi's Phoenix: a city nourished by suffering, a garden built on dust.",
      "resource scarcity has gone from threat to defining condition. it is not inconceivable that this century will see hydrological bankruptcy across several nations. the emptying of the basin reveals more than climate consequence; it lays bare the moral drought of a civilization unwilling to reckon with its own excess.",
